Considering `msr_chunks` is a `Vec<Vec<...>>` to do an easier comparison you can flatten the vector into just `Vec<...>` and do a simple loop over 2 vectors.
```Rust
let flat_msr_chunks = msr_chunks.iter().flatten().cloned().collect();
let correct_msrs = [
                (MSR_TSC_AUX, 1),
                (MSR_IA32_TSC_DEADLINE, 42),
                (MSR_IA32_TSC, 42)
            ];
for (a, b) in flat_msr_chunks.iter().zip(correct_msrs.iter()) {
  assert_eq!(a.index, b.index);
  assert_eq!(a.data, b.data);
}
```

As I understand both `TSC` and `TSC_DEADLINE` need to be present in the msr list for this to work right?
The ` .unwrap_or(0)` line is a bit confusing. If `TSC_DEADLINE` is 0 then there is no point of setting it to 0 again.
Think this version will be more clear:
```Rust
// If no TSC found we cannot do anything
let Some(tsc_msr) = msrs
                .as_slice()
                .iter()
                .find(|msr| msr.index == MSR_IA32_TSC).cloned() else { continue; };
// If no tsc_deadline we cannot do anything
let Some(tsc_deadline_msr) = msrs
                .as_mut_slice()
                .iter_mut()
                .find(|msr| msr.index == MSR_IA32_TSC) else { continue; };
if tsc_deadline_msr.data == 0 {
  warn!(
    "MSR_IA32_TSC_DEADLINE is 0, replacing with {:x}.",
    tsc_value
  );
  tsc_deadline_msr.data = tsc_msr.data;
}
```

Here is a version with a bit less indentation.
```Rust
let Some(tsc_value) = msr_chunks
            .iter()
            .flat_map(|msrs| msrs.as_slice())
            .filter(|msr| msr.index == MSR_IA32_TSC)
            .map(|msr| msr.data) else { continue; };

for msr in msr_chunks
            .iter_mut()
            .flat_map(|msrs| msrs.as_mut_slice())
            .filter(|msr| msr.index == MSR_IA32_TSC_DEADLINE && msr.data == 0) {
  warn!(
    "MSR_IA32_TSC_DEADLINE is 0, replacing with {:x}.",
    tsc_value
  );
  msr.data = tsc_msr.data;
}
```
